=== Introduction
--- Our Fathers Have Told Us [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 44
sub: Our fathers have told us • For thy sake are we killed all the day long • Arise for our help
refs: Psalm 44:1; Psalm 44:22; Psalm 44:26
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Our fathers have told us
--- The sons of Korah
kicker: Psalm 44 · A congregational lament
p: **Psalm 44** is attributed to *the sons of Korah*, a Levitical family of temple musicians and gatekeepers. Faithful singers, who are credited with 12 Psalms in total, 42-49 and 84-85, 87-88, across multiple generations.
p: **Psalm 44** itself is thought to be written during the Assyrian invasion and their siege of Jerusalem and general destruction of the Southern Kingdom of Judea.
p: It follows a similar pattern of congregational laments, as we saw in **Psalm 42-43**, and stresses the victories of the past that God has won for His people, while calling out for deliverance in the present.

--- What work thou didst in their days
kicker: Psalm 44 · Our fathers have told us
big: Psalm 44:1 | mark: our fathers have told us, what work thou didst in their days
p: It opens with an acknowledgement of their knowledge of God's grace and might for His people, passed down by their fathers.
p: *Our fathers have told us, what work thou didst in their days*. The importance of generational evangelism is incredibly important, for obvious reasons, we should tell our children about the importance of God moving in our lives, so that they may learn to trust in Him during hard times, and not be dismayed when tribulation comes.

--- When thy son asketh thee
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:1
p: But the practice of children asking and fathers telling was legally commanded in the Law of Moses to ensure that the memory of the Exodus from Egypt was never forgotten.
scripture: Deuteronomy 6:18-20 | mark: when thy son asketh thee in time to come

--- Then thou shalt say unto thy son
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:1
scripture: Deuteronomy 6:21-23 | mark: Then thou shalt say unto thy son

--- Because thou hadst a favour unto them
kicker: Psalm 44 · Not by their own sword
scripture: Psalm 44:2-3 | mark: they got not the land in possession by their own sword; because thou hadst a favour unto them
p: The Psalm is not only stating their education as a fact, but is telling God in their prayer that their fathers obeyed and they listened, following His decree. That they were obedient in this thing He had said.
p: The victories they were given by His power and promise and by the light of His countenance - His divine grace towards them, is remembered and appreciated. That their fathers didn't trust their own swords, or their own strength, but wholly relied on the Lord, *because thou hadst a favour unto them*.

--- Thou art my King, O God
kicker: Psalm 44 · Command deliverances
scripture: Psalm 44:4-8 | mark: Thou art my King, O God: command deliverances for Jacob; For I will not trust in my bow
p: When we are in straits, and feeling hopeless, the remembrance of past deliverance, coupled with the thankful acknowledgement to the Lord for these things, leads the prayer, and gives weight to the petition for help in the present distress.
p: Thou art our Lord, command deliverance to thy people.

=== But thou hast cast off
--- A communal prayer
kicker: Psalm 44 · God's people
p: While individual prayers can and should follow this pattern of recognition and thanksgiving of past actions, before requesting aid for the current troubles, this Psalm is specifically a communal prayer for God's people. Faithful Israel, the nation in the past, spiritual Israel today.
p: Collectively, Christians have faced persecution and tribulations, by the enemies of Christ. 1st century persecution by the Jews and the Romans, the Dark Ages martyrdom of believers at the hands of the Roman Catholic Church. The mass murder by Muslims against Nigerian Christians today.
p: The same will be suffered in the future, when the beast makes war on the saints during Tribulation.

--- Hypothetical steadfastness
kicker: Application • Tribulation
p: American Christians today may posture and pose about their hypothetical steadfastness during a supposed tribulation in their lifetime. That they will gladly offer their necks to the sword for Jesus. Boasting of their own willingness to sacrifice themselves as martyrs for the promised crowns in the post-trib pre-wrath time to come.
p: Or, like a vast majority of the brethren, believe fervently that they will actually be spared any persecution in the end times by being whisked away before it even starts. The pre-trib wishful thinking that dominates Christian churches today, leads to a false comfort that political solutions will forestall the inevitable that they've excused themselves from, rather than repentance and prayer, soul-winning and standards.

--- Thou hast cast off
kicker: Psalm 44 · Like sheep appointed for meat
scripture: Psalm 44:9-12 | mark: But thou hast cast off, and put us to shame; Thou hast given us like sheep appointed for meat
p: But either group may look upon the persecuted saints today, or the congregation at the time of **Psalm 44**, and see their plaintive questions against God, and His seeming abandonment of His people, and look down our noses at it.
p: "If it were us, in our day, we wouldn't blame God for allowing those that hate us to spoil for themselves! We wouldn't question why God has cast us off, and left us to die!"

--- A byword among the heathen
kicker: Psalm 44 · A reproach to our neighbours
scripture: Psalm 44:13-16 | mark: Thou makest us a byword among the heathen
p: The same Christians who cry out in pain at every "Happy holidays!" and sit on their couches when there are doors to knock and souls to save. Who prioritize football and recreation over church. Who bemoan the wickedness of the world, while living exactly like it.

--- The fiery trial
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:9–16
scripture: 1 Peter 4:12-14 | mark: think it not strange concerning the fiery trial
p: If the people who call themselves by His name were naturally like Stephen, standing up for the faith and being willing to suffer death for the truth while forgiving those that stoned him to death, the New Testament wouldn't be so full of warnings and encouragement.

=== For thy sake
--- Counted as sheep for the slaughter
kicker: Psalm 44 · Awake, why sleepest thou?
p: In **Psalm 44:22–24** they cry out desperately to God;
scripture: Psalm 44:22-24 | mark: for thy sake are we killed all the day long; Awake, why sleepest thou, O Lord?
p: The same lament is turned to edifying instruction in the New Testament;

--- More than conquerors
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:22
scripture: Romans 8:35-37 | mark: As it is written, For thy sake we are killed all the day long; more than conquerors
p: Because man is naturally going to complain in the best of times, we need to be prepared for the proper attitude in the worst. To be persuaded that nothing can separate us from the love of Christ. Even when things are bleak.

--- Be thou faithful unto death
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:22
p: Jesus Himself gave this encouragement to the church in Smyrna, who were facing real persecution at the hands of their enemies.
scripture: Revelation 2:8-11 | mark: be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life

--- Grown fat off His blessings
kicker: Application • Tribulation
p: Just as the instructions to live separated lives, to be holy as He is holy, is necessary because man does not by his fleshly nature do good works, even those who are saved, the same flesh tends to need reminders of past victory, while still may cry out "Why?" to their Saviour.
p: Righteous Job did. The best man of his generation, and yet he sought answers from God, and sinned not with his mouth while doing so, according to scripture.
p: Modern American Christians, made weak by the comforts and bounty God has graciously given our nation, grown fat off His blessings, will most assuredly cry out the same laments when the abomination of desolation is rolled out, and the armies of the beast strike us down.

--- How long, O Lord?
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 44:23
p: Even the spirits of the martyrs in Great Tribulation will cry out, impatiently.
scripture: Revelation 6:9-10 | mark: How long, O Lord, holy and true
p: We naturally may feel abandoned in the thick of it. Crying out, as the congregation does, is sadly expected.
p: *Awake, why sleepest thou, O Lord? arise, cast us not off for ever. Wherefore hidest thou thy face, and forgettest our affliction and our oppression?*

--- Bowed down to the dust
kicker: Psalm 44 · Bowed down
big: Psalm 44:25 | mark: our soul is bowed down to the dust
p: We may be beaten down, bowed to the dust, despondent.
p: But remember the stories told by our fathers. Remember the victories God has given us. Remember that we have already overcome in Christ. That nothing will separate us from His love.
p: We may turn to the Lord in fear. We may be consumed by our enemies, and feel forgotten. We may be impatient in the face of the tribulation.

--- Arise for our help
kicker: Psalm 44 · For thy mercies' sake
p: But the key is to turn towards God in our prayers.
big: Psalm 44:26
center: Amen.

=== Yet have we not forgotten thee
--- Their innocence
kicker: Psalm 44 · Not dealt falsely
scripture: Psalm 44:17-21 | mark: yet have we not forgotten thee, neither have we dealt falsely in thy covenant; Shall not God search this out?
p: What makes **Psalm 44** so jarring and often makes modern readers uncomfortable, is that the congregation is stating they are not being punished for apostasy or adultery or for being unfaithful.
p: In many other laments, Israel confesses their sins and acknowledges that their defeat is a righteous judgment from God. But here, they boldly declare their innocence:

--- Travails come upon the just
kicker: Psalm 44 · The faithful
p: Travails come upon the just. Upon the faithful who have placed their trust in the Lord.
p: The psalmists raise a relevant point today; believers may cry out to God and ask why they are suffering, knowing they have not fallen to apostasy.
p: These sons of Korah are believers, and believers cannot apostatize. They feel confident in asking God to search their hearts for any sign of adultery in worship of Him.

--- Still faithful
kicker: Psalm 44 · The faithful
p: They may, amongst themselves, talk about how the northern kingdom of Israel which was also being afflicted in this time by the Assyrian empire, deserved it. They had fallen into the sin of Jeroboam and gone after strange gods.
p: But these faithful Levites could wonder what they themselves may have done to bring upon these attacks.
p: Whatever other sins they may have had in their lives or collectively, they still were faithful to the Lord.

--- Paul quotes Psalm 44
kicker: Psalm 44 · Past lament, future hope
p: They did exactly what Deuteronomy 6 commanded. They listened to their fathers. They trusted not in their own bows or swords. They kept the faith, yet they were still *scattered among the heathen* and *counted as sheep for the slaughter*, and yet still faced tribulation.
p: Paul quotes **Psalm 44** in **Romans** for exactly this reason. To show that God eventually does deliver the faithful. That we can count on him even when it seems like the entire world is against us.
p: Paul is using the same technique of teaching to the children the stories of the fathers, to bring us hope. Judah was delivered from the Assyrians. And the faithful were delivered by the promise of Christ being fulfilled.

--- Stand fast
kicker: Psalm 44 · Past lament, future hope
p: Using then a past lament to give future hope, and show the strength of God in keeping His promises is the entire point of the commandment.
p: We see the Deliverance of God's people into the promised land in the past used as hope for the people then present Jerusalem, and their cries are then given to us as hope today knowing that they were eventually delivered.
p: If we are scattered to the wilderness, to the place of dragons, or are led through the valley of the shadow of death, we have that hope within us. The call to have the patience of Job. The faith of the fathers. The understanding of Paul.
p: So stand fast and comfort one another with these words.

